extreme  in marin county mandatory water  restrictions have been imposed  and greg milam has been seeing the  impact of the drought  at phoenix lake reservoir north of san  francisco  at the sharp end of the very real  climate crisis  this is phoenix lake up here north of  san francisco this is one of the seven  reservoirs that supplies  almost all of the water to this part of  california known as marin county and you  don't need to be an expert to look out  there and see what's going on the water  level in this reservoir  they say is about 30 of where it should  be in a normal year  you look up those caked banks of mud  there cracked and the  heat here and the water should be up at  the level of that  that the tree line there at the level of  the grass but it is that extreme  situation historic drought here they say  is playing out just as an illustration  where i'm standing in a normal year my  feet would be  under water the water would would lap up  over the edge of the reservoir into this  reed bed and down into the spillway  across this region  the reservoirs are at 50 percent and  that's why they've taken this drastic  action they say of mandatory water  restrictions  on the 200 000 people who live in marin  county they can't power wash their  houses or driveways they can't wash  their cars at home  they can't fill their swimming pools and  hot tubs  because they say this action is needed  ahead of of course what's to come which  will be the hot dry months  of summer and when you look at what the  us government scientists are saying  about the climate change future of  america for a lot of people here  it's already a modern day reality in  today's other climate news the uk  and india have announced a joint  agreement to combat climate change the  countries will work together in areas  including clean energy  and protecting forests it comes as  foreign ministers from the world's  leading economies are taking part in  face-to-face meetings at the g7 summit  in london  the group will discuss how to help  vulnerable countries cope with the  impact of climate change  [Applause]  politicians in france are due to vote on  a new climate law which has been  criticized by environmental groups  it includes measures that would ban some  domestic flights  and put restrictions on renting out  badly insulated houses  campaigners say the bill doesn't go far  enough given the pace  of climate change globally  and uber is working with startup company  arrival  to produce electric taxis the two  companies want to create a greener car  that will use uber's app  to automatically match passengers with  cars that have  sufficient battery range arrivals senior  vice president tom elvidge  says they aim to start producing the  taxis by the end  of 2023. now you may remember  last month 40 world leaders came  together a president biden's  climate change summit and the pledges  that were made there  including new targets on cutting  emissions have changed  how much scientists think our world will  warm by the end of the century  so here's where we are at the moment at  just above 1.2 degrees of  warming and that number is going up all  the time and scientists estimate that a  current rate  this is the amount of time left until we  hit  1.5 degrees so here's the updated  climate action  tracker predictions for the rest of the  century and they estimate that  if there's full implementation of the  net zero targets that have been  announced or  being considered global warming by 2100  could be as low as two degrees now  that's the optimistic target  scenario and is still far above the 1.5  degrees committed to  at the paris agreement in 2015.  if we then look at current pledges and  targets those are  unconditional commitments that  governments have made and officially  submitted to the un  it's thought that there'll be 2.4  degrees of warming by 2100 that's  0.2 degrees cooler than before the biden  summit  now if no change was made to policies  that are currently in place around the  world it's estimated that global warming  will be as high as nearly  three degrees by the end of the century  now phasing out coal as a power source  is going to be crucial in keeping those  global temperature rises  below 1.5 degrees but a new report has  found  the british bank's financial support for  companies involved in the coal industry  has actually increased since the 2015  paris agreement so uk investors provided  loans and underwriting services worth  nearly  22 billion pounds to coal companies  during 2019. now that's an increase of  nearly 16 billion pounds  compared with figures for 2016 and it  makes the uk the third  largest investor in the coal industry  after the united states and japan  and many british banks have pledged to  address their climate policies  and stop financing coal projects despite  that  reclaimed finance says the uk's top 10  financial firms are responsible for 78  of the country's total coal investments  well our business correspondent paul  kelso  joins me now so paul just how much  pressure  are banks under to reduce their  investments in coal projects  there's political pressure we heard  secretary of state anthony blinken tell  economies that were still investing in  new coal that they would be in his words  hearing from the united states  but there's pressure around the way coal  is financed  coming in the shape of this report and  indeed from the bank's  own commitments to green not just their  businesses but their investments and the  reason people are looking at the city of  london  is because no matter what you burn power  generators need money and the city of  london is  built on great reserves of finance and  how that finance  is deployed will have a great impact on  whether climate commitments can be  met now this report shows that british  banks are investing still  heavily in coal around the world  bangladesh indonesia south korea  korea some of it indeed going into  companies that the campaigners say  fund new coal developments which is  something coming at a time when  there's general agreement coal has to be  phased out but half of that funding is  coming via barclays like many banks  it has committed to greening its  investment book but it has said  that the best way to do that is to fund  the transition to clean  energy rather than just withdrawing the  funding  to coal power now campaigners will say  that's quite a  good way of also ensuring that balance  sheets at banks  don't get hit and ultimately  shareholders and indeed perhaps  investors  won't lose out the truth is that as long  as there is coal being  generated as long as there is profit as  well as power  in funding coal the city of london and  other financial centres  are going to retain an interest in  investing in it  paul thank you now when you think of  nature helping to tackle climate change  forests full of trees sucking in carbon  dioxide  probably come to mind but far more  carbon is stored in the seas around the  uk  than in the forests on land and a new  report published today  is calling on the government to treat  re-wilding the oceans  with the same level of urgency as  reforestation  here's why the sea is so important in  fighting climate change  in 60 seconds  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  foreign  [Applause]  [Music]  so  [Music]  well joining me now is marine  conservation analyst kelly forsythe  so kelly forsyth just how important are  our oceans for storing carbon  often when people think about carbon  storage the first thing that comes to  mind is forests  and whilst forests all over the world um  do play an important role in carbon  storage so does the ocean  so the oceans cover about 70 percent of  the earth's surface  our oceans are actually the world's  largest carbon sinks  the ocean and its ecosystems such as  seagrass meadows  marshes and mangrove forests play a  really vital role in capturing carbon  under actually known as blue carbon  systems however  the oceans are also home to tiny  microscopic plants called phytoplankton  so like other plants phytoplankton are  able to change carbon dioxide into  oxygen  through a process known as  photosynthesis this is particularly  important as carbon dioxide is a  greenhouse gas  meaning that it contributes  significantly to global warming  sea surface temperature and ultimately  climate change  these tiny plants are the backbone of  the marine food chain  and end up being eaten by zooplankton or  tiny microscopic animals which then  get eaten by fish and the chain goes on  and on  eventually resulting in about 10 billion  tons of carbon  being transferred from the atmosphere  into the different layers of the ocean  so phytoplankton alone are responsible  for providing around 50  of the oxygen in our atmosphere which is  around the same amount as all of the  plants  on land now before we go we've just got  time to show you the real-time power mix  in great britain  and today a huge 63 of electricity is  coming from renewable  sources at the moment and that's in part  because of the blustery weather that  we've been seeing across the country  according to the national grid a new  wind power record was set yesterday  with 17.6 gigawatts of power generated  providing nearly 50  of electricity and that's everything  from us  for today on the daily climate show  tomorrow find out how much  limiting global warming to 1.5 degrees  could affect sea levels around the world  see you then  you

